1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,934
AI video generators now produce minutes of
footage from a single prompt.

2
00:00:05,994 --> 00:00:07,799
They also fail quietly.

3
00:00:07,859 --> 00:00:10,596
A prop changes design between shots.

4
00:00:10,656 --> 00:00:13,859
A line of dialogue comes out wrong.

5
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:18,987
Nobody notices until a human scrubs every
second of every render.

6
00:00:19,047 --> 00:00:24,582
And asking a second model, "does this look
fine?", is not evidence.

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:31,522
ProofFrame is an independent verification
layer that sits between the approved brief

8
00:00:31,582 --> 00:00:33,067
and the generator.

9
00:00:33,127 --> 00:00:34,097
Three rules.

10
00:00:34,157 --> 00:00:35,643
One: blind observation.

11
00:00:35,703 --> 00:00:41,309
The checking model never sees the expected
answer while it watches.

12
00:00:41,369 --> 00:00:42,339
Two: fail-closed.

13
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:48,521
If a check cannot be verified, it is
unresolved, never a pass.

14
00:00:48,581 --> 00:00:54,702
Three: every finding is tied to frames and
timestamps, so a human,

15
00:00:54,762 --> 00:00:58,823
or the generator, can act on it immediately.

16
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,753
Give ProofFrame the film and its contract,

17
00:01:02,813 --> 00:01:06,167
the locked script compiled into checkable
assertions,

18
00:01:06,227 --> 00:01:09,092
and it verifies shot by shot.

19
00:01:09,152 --> 00:01:11,530
This handle must stay folded.

20
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:12,506
It doesn't.

21
00:01:12,566 --> 00:01:14,944
This lantern must not change.

22
00:01:15,004 --> 00:01:15,919
It does.

23
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:21,283
Dialogue is checked too, by two independent
transcribers: Muse Voice Transcribe,

24
00:01:21,343 --> 00:01:27,134
and ElevenLabs Scribe, which we use because
it handles Vietnamese dialogue better.

25
00:01:27,194 --> 00:01:29,085
Neither sees the script.

26
00:01:29,145 --> 00:01:30,548
When they disagree,

27
00:01:30,608 --> 00:01:35,424
the line is flagged with exactly what each
one heard.

28
00:01:35,484 --> 00:01:39,325
This full-episode contract run cost eight
dollars thirty.

29
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:40,828
No brief?

30
00:01:40,888 --> 00:01:46,264
Drop in a video and ProofFrame scans it on
its own:

31
00:01:46,324 --> 00:01:49,723
generation artifacts, cross-shot continuity
breaks, technical defects.

32
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:54,664
It is honest about what a scan means:
sampled evidence,

33
00:01:54,724 --> 00:01:56,641
not a script check.

34
00:01:56,701 --> 00:02:02,571
On this eight-minute draft the scan cost two
dollars ninety-one and confirmed

35
00:02:02,631 --> 00:02:03,559
twenty-four defects,

36
00:02:03,619 --> 00:02:09,489
including a handle design flip in the
opening shots that two human

37
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:11,466
review passes had missed.

38
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:16,856
Findings are structured, so they feed
straight back to production.

39
00:02:16,916 --> 00:02:21,871
This line lost its first word at an edit
boundary.

40
00:02:21,931 --> 00:02:23,877
Both transcribers confirmed it.

41
00:02:23,937 --> 00:02:29,896
Production fixed the trim, we re-ran, and
the same check now passes.

42
00:02:29,956 --> 00:02:35,914
Fix exactly what is flagged, re-run, and the
result is classified: fixed,

43
00:02:35,974 --> 00:02:37,921
still failing, or regressed.

44
00:02:37,981 --> 00:02:39,425
No full re-watch.

45
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:44,063
Built solo during the hackathon on Muse
Spark,

46
00:02:44,123 --> 00:02:49,869
through the Meta Model API, with ElevenLabs
Scribe for dialogue transcription.

47
00:02:49,929 --> 00:02:55,676
It is tested in production on a Vietnamese
AI-generated school-mystery series.
